99 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (This event was built around one simple idea: every student in the Kandy zone deserves a real chance to try robotics, regardless of whether their school already has a robotics club, a lab, or prior experience. That's why the bootcamp comes before the competition. to make sure every team starts from the same place, with the same foundation, and the same shot at doing well.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Over the next few months you'll learn to wire sensors, write logic that makes real-time decisions, and build a robot that can think for itself on a course it's never seen before. You'll hit problems you didn't expect - a sensor that misreads a color, a motor that doesn't respond the way your code assumes it should, a robot that gets stuck where you swore it wouldn't. That's normal. Every team in this competition will run into something like it.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (What matters is what you do next. You'll debug it, test it again, and get it working and that process, not the trophy at the end, is the actual point of this event.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Robotics rewards patience and curiosity far more than it rewards getting things right on the first try. If you walk away from Robo-Invent 2026 having learned how to take a problem apart and put a working solution back together, you've already gotten the most valuable thing this event has to offer.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (We're glad you're here. Read this guidebook carefully, ask questions early, lean on your bootcamp mentors when you're stuck, and enjoy building something that actually works.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
>>
